# Copy to .env to override. Everything here is optional.
#
#   cp .env.example .env

# Port on 127.0.0.1 where the app is published. The loopback interface is not
# configurable: it is what keeps your report off the local network.
UNBAGGED_PORT=8420

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reading a response that arrived as screen captures
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Captures are read by tesseract, which ships in the image and needs no setting.
# Everything below is about the SECOND reader: a local vision model, asked only
# about the pages the first one could not read into a basket that adds up.
#
# On the response this was built for that is one capture out of 46, so leaving
# all of this unset costs one visit's contents, not the response.
#
# Off unless UNBAGGED_OLLAMA_HOST is set. Setting it means an image of a receipt
# — your shopping, the shop, the hour, the last digits of your card — is sent to
# whatever is listening there. On this machine that is between you and your own
# computer. Anywhere else it is not, which is what the third setting is about.
#
# UNBAGGED_OLLAMA_HOST=http://localhost:11434
#
# Which model answers. It defaults to minicpm-v4.5:8b and only needs setting to
# ask for a different one. That default is measured rather than chosen: twelve
# vision models over six receipt-shaped pages, scored through this lane, written
# up in src/unbagged/adapters/hmart/NOTES.md. Nothing in the field reads more of
# a page than it does, and of the three that read as much it is the fastest — so
# there is no accuracy-for-speed trade to make here. The models that cost two to
# three times as much per page do not read better. Pull it before you point at
# it: `ollama pull minicpm-v4.5:8b`.
# UNBAGGED_OLLAMA_VISION_MODEL=minicpm-v4.5:8b
#
# Required before a host that is NOT this machine will be used at all. There is
# no default that reaches one, and an address the parser cannot read counts as
# remote rather than local.
# UNBAGGED_ALLOW_REMOTE_OLLAMA=false
#
# Running in the container and the model on your machine: the container's
# "localhost" is the container. Use http://host.docker.internal:11434, which
# docker-compose.yml already maps, and acknowledge it above — it is not this
# machine as far as the app can tell, and that is the honest answer.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What the machine running Ollama needs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# All of this is about the host serving the model, which may not be this one.
# The app never loads a model itself, so none of it applies to the container
# when the model lives somewhere else.
#
# Weights. An 8B model at Ollama's own quantisation is a few gigabytes, and it
# stays resident between calls once loaded. Ask your host rather than trusting a
# figure written here: `ollama show minicpm-v4.5:8b` for what it pulled, and
# `ollama ps` for what that occupies loaded.
#
# Context. A call asks for 8192 tokens plus 3072 for each page it sends, and
# doubles that up to twice more if the server says the prompt overran — so one
# page can end up asking for roughly 45k. That window is KV cache sitting on top
# of the weights. Somewhere around 8GB of VRAM, or of unified memory on an Apple
# machine, holds the pair without spilling to the CPU. Less than that still
# works and is slower.
#
# Time. About eight seconds a page on a GPU holding the model; CPU-only is
# minutes. Each call gives up at 180s, which a warm GPU host is nowhere near and
# a cold CPU host can genuinely hit — a model measured at 40s a page in the
# bake-off timed out on one. The first call after an idle period also pays the
# load, so if pages arrive minutes apart, keep the model resident with
# OLLAMA_KEEP_ALIVE rather than paying it every time.
#
# How much of this gets used. One call per capture the deterministic reader
# could not make add up — not one per capture, and not one per response. On the
# response this was built for, that is one page out of 46.
